\subsection{Cross-Scale Analysis}
\label{sec:gen:crossscale}

The preceding analyses use 1--1.5B parameter models.
To test whether depth signal persists in substantially larger generators, we extend the benchmark to Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct~\cite{qwen2.5} under 4-bit NF4 QLoRA fine-tuning, generating depths $d \in \{0, 1, \ldots, 5\}$ with nucleus sampling ($p{=}0.95$, 500 samples per depth).
Feature extraction uses the same reference model (GPT-2 base, 124M) and 19-feature pipeline as all prior experiments.

% TODO: Add Llama-3-8B results when available

\paragraph{Scale comparison.}
Table~\ref{tab:cross_scale} contrasts Qwen2.5-7B with the three 1--1.5B models on matched evaluation (nucleus sampling, depths 0--3).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Cross-scale comparison: 4-class RF accuracy (\%, depths 0--3, nucleus) and $\mathrm{EDD}_{T{=}0.70}$ (depths 0--5). CI: 95\% bootstrap. Chance = 25\%.}
\label{tab:cross_scale}
\begin{tabular}{@{}lcccc@{}}
\toprule
& Qwen2.5-7B & Pythia-1.4B & OLMo-1B & GPT-2~XL \\
\midrule
4-class acc & 60.1\,[58.0, 62.1] & 78.2 & 78.8 & 83.3 \\
$\mathrm{EDD}_{T{=}0.70}$ & ${>}5$ & 4 & 4 & 4 \\
d0-vs-d1 AUC & 0.969 & 0.998 & 0.997 & 0.999 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Three findings emerge from experiment \texttt{cross\_scale\_qwen7b\_001}:

\paragraph{(1) Depth signal persists at 7B scale.}
Four-class accuracy of 60.1\% (95\% CI $[58.0, 62.1]$) exceeds the 25\% chance baseline by $2.4\times$, confirming that recursive depth remains estimable from perplexity-based features even when the generator is 5--7$\times$ larger than the reference model.
Depth-0 separability is preserved: all pairwise AUCs involving d0 exceed 0.92 (d0v1$\,{=}\,$0.921, d0v2$\,{=}\,$0.933, d0v3$\,{=}\,$0.936), comparable to the near-perfect d0 separation observed at 1--1.5B.

\paragraph{(2) EDD increases with model scale.}
At threshold $T{=}0.70$, all consecutive-pair AUCs remain above 0.70 through depth~5 (Table~\ref{tab:cross_scale_auc}), yielding $\mathrm{EDD}_{T{=}0.70} > 5$---exceeding the $\mathrm{EDD}{=}4$ observed for all three 1--1.5B models at the same threshold.
The 7B generator produces weaker per-step signal (mean consecutive AUC 0.782 vs.\ 0.876 for 1--1.5B) but sustains it across more recursive iterations before saturation.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Consecutive binary AUC for Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct (nucleus $p{=}0.95$). All pairs exceed $T{=}0.70$; the non-monotonic pattern (d2v3 $<$ d3v4 $<$ d4v5) contrasts with the monotonic decay at 1--1.5B scale.}
\label{tab:cross_scale_auc}
\begin{tabular}{@{}lccccc@{}}
\toprule
Pair & d0v1 & d1v2 & d2v3 & d3v4 & d4v5 \\
\midrule
AUC & 0.969 & 0.729 & 0.709 & 0.732 & 0.772 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\paragraph{(3) Non-monotonic AUC trajectory.}
Unlike the monotonic AUC decay observed at 1--1.5B scale (d0v1 $>$ d1v2 $>$ d2v3 $>$ d3v4 $>$ d4v5), the 7B profile exhibits a U-shaped recovery beyond d2v3: consecutive AUC drops from 0.969 (d0v1) to a minimum of 0.709 (d2v3), then \emph{rises} to 0.732 (d3v4) and 0.772 (d4v5).
We hypothesize this reflects \emph{formulaic degradation} in instruction-tuned models at high recursive depth: as generation quality collapses, outputs become increasingly stereotyped and template-like, producing distinctive statistical signatures that are paradoxically \emph{easier} to distinguish from the preceding depth than the subtler d1/d2/d3 transitions.

\paragraph{Caveats.}
The CI lower bound (58.0\%) does not exclude sub-60\% accuracy, placing the absolute performance closer to the boundary of practical utility than the 1--1.5B results.
The non-monotonic AUC pattern means the EDD$\,{>}\,$5 finding relies partly on the formulaic-degradation effect at high depths rather than a uniformly strong signal.
Furthermore, the instruction-tuned nature of Qwen2.5-7B introduces a confound absent in the base-model experiments: RLHF/DPO alignment may independently shape the feature trajectory in ways not attributable to recursive depth alone.
Whether these patterns replicate across other 7B+ architectures (e.g., Llama-3-8B) is under active investigation.

